  5	//! `release_forge_publish_e2e_tests.rs` and `durable_release_e2e_tests.rs`
  6	//! cover npm and PyPI publish only against fake providers. This file adds a
  7	//! real local Verdaccio registry to the mix: `callisto release execute`'s
  8	//! actual npm provider adapter, real `npm`, real HTTP over loopback. The
  9	//! fake-provider suites are unchanged and remain the fast, hermetic default.
 10	//!
 11	//! Registry routing goes entirely through npm's own `.npmrc` (a project-root
 12	//! file, gitignored so it never dirties the release-trust worktree check),
 13	//! never a callisto registry binding: `registry_endpoint::canonical_registry_url`
 14	//! rejects non-https URLs with no loopback exception, by design, so a local `http://127.0.0.1` test
 15	//! registry can never be configured as a `[registries]` entry. Leaving the
 16	//! package unmatched by any `[registries]` override keeps
 17	//! `PreparedRegistryBinding.endpoint` at `None`, which is also what makes
 18	//! this reachable at all: both `npm view` (observation) and `npm publish`
 19	//! omit `--registry` whenever `endpoint` is `None`, so npm resolves the
 20	//! registry itself from `.npmrc` -- unlike PyPI's observation step, which
 21	//! hardcodes the public simple index when `endpoint` is `None` (see the PyPI
 22	//! note below).
 23	//!
 24	//! PyPI drives `registry_argv::pypi_publish_argv` directly (the exact
 25	//! production build-then-upload argv, including `--repository-url` for a
 26	//! private index) rather than through `callisto release execute`: that CLI
 27	//! path routes a registry through `PreparedRegistryBinding`, whose
 28	//! `canonical_registry_url` rejects non-https with no loopback exception --
